#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050492 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184159 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184063 :question/problem "Find $B^2$ given that \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Computing $B^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since $B^2=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184749 :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${B}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${B}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{5} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{3} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {B}^{3} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{5}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184843 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${A}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${A}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $A$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${A}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265941 :question/problem "If $T=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${T}^{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${T}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{2} & =T\\cdot T \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${T}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $T$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${T}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{6} & ={T}^{2}\\cdot {T}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {T}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{T}^{6}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265944 :question/problem "If $K=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 27 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${K}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${K}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{2} & =K\\cdot K \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 27 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 27 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${K}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $K$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${K}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{2} & ={K}^{2}\\cdot {K}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348090 :question/problem "If $R=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${R}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & =R\\cdot R \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${R}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $R$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${R}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{5} & ={R}^{2}\\cdot {R}^{3} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {R}^{3} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{R}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378643 :question/problem "If $S=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${S}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${S}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =S\\cdot S \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${S}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $S$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${S}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{3} & ={S}^{2}\\cdot S \\\\  & =O\\cdot S \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378650 :question/problem "If $R=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${R}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & =R\\cdot R \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${R}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $R$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${R}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & ={R}^{2}\\cdot {R}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{R}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380090 :question/problem "If $V=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${V}^{7}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${V}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{2} & =V\\cdot V \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${V}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $V$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${V}^{7}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{7} & ={V}^{2}\\cdot {V}^{5} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {V}^{5} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{7}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388118 :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${B}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${B}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{3}$ quickly, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{3} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =O\\cdot B \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n{B}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388125 :question/problem "If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${C}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${C}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${C}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $C$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${C}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot {C}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{C}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393828 :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 25 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${B}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 25 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 25 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${B}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{6} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {B}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{6}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393870 :question/problem "If $K=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${K}^{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${K}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{2} & =K\\cdot K \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${K}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $K$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${K}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{6} & ={K}^{2}\\cdot {K}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {K}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{6}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184064 :question/problem "Find $C^5$ given that\n\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute $C^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $C^2=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute $C^5$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{5} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot {C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n\n\n$$\nC^5=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184765 :question/problem "If $B = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, then $B^{5} =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184766 :question/problem "If $E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${E}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${E}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{2} & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${E}^{2}=E,$ so $E$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${E}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{4} & ={E}^{2}\\cdot {E}^{2} \\\\  & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & ={E}^{2} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{E}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265951 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${A}^{2}=A,$ so $A$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${A}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & ={A}^{2} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348101 :question/problem "If $W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${W}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${W}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{2} & =W\\cdot W \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${W}^{2}=W,$ so $W$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${W}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{4} & ={W}^{2}\\cdot {W}^{2} \\\\  & =W\\cdot W \\\\  & ={W}^{2} \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{W}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375916 :question/problem "If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${C}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${C}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${C}^{2}=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix.  \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${C}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{4} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot {C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{C}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377735 :question/problem "If $U=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${U}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${U}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{2} & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${U}^{2}=U,$ so $U$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${U}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{3} & ={U}^{2}\\cdot U \\\\  & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & ={U}^{2} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{U}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379372 :question/problem "If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${C}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${C}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${C}^{2}=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${C}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{3} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379379 :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${B}^{2}=B,$ so $B$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{4} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{2} \\\\  & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{B}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380097 :question/problem "If \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${P}^{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${P}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{2} & =P\\cdot P \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${P}^{2}=P,$ so $P$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${P}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{6} & ={P}^{2}\\cdot {P}^{2}\\cdot {P}^{2} \\\\  & =P\\cdot P\\cdot P \\\\  & ={P}^{2}\\cdot P \\\\  & =P\\cdot P \\\\  & ={P}^{2} \\\\  & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{P}^{6}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390750 :question/problem "If \n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${D}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${D}^{2}=D,$ so $D$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${D}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{3} & ={D}^{2}\\cdot D \\\\  & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & ={D}^{2} \\\\  & =D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{D}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391958 :question/problem "If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${C}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${C}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${C}^{2}=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${C}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{3} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{C}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393912 :question/problem "If $Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${Z}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${Z}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{2} & =Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${Z}^{2}=Z,$ so $Z$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${Z}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{5} & ={Z}^{2}\\cdot {Z}^{2}\\cdot Z \\\\  & =Z\\cdot Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & ={Z}^{2}\\cdot Z \\\\  & =Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & ={Z}^{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Z}^{5}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421545 :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${B}^{2}=B,$ so $B$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{5} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =B\\cdot B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & ={B}^{2} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{B}^{5}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440932 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${A}^{2}=A,$ so $A$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${A}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & ={A}^{2} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}]}] :topic/title "Nilpotent and Idempotent Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050727 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059552 :question/problem "Find $C^T,$ if \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$C =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n\n$$\nC^T =\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059967 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$Y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nthen ${Y}^{T}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{Y}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 9 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059993 :question/problem "Find $A^{T}$, given that\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n." :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns.\n\n$$\nA^T=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240846 :question/problem "Find ${Y}^{T}$, given that \n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$Y=\\begin{bmatrix}-20 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 9 & 6 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{Y}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-20 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 6 \\\\ 15 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262587 :question/problem "Find ${G}^{T}$, given that \n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$G=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{G}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262588 :question/problem "Find ${Q}^{T}$, given that \n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{Q}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262589 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$H=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen ${H}^{T}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{H}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262590 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen ${B}^{T}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{B}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295249 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $C.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${C}^{T}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-5}}</td><td>{{field-6}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295250 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n,\nthen find the transpose of $E.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${E}^{T}=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>{{field-5}}</td><td>{{field-6}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-7}}</td><td>{{field-8}}</td><td>{{field-9}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n\n$$\n{E}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295251 :question/problem "If\n    \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-ope